From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 11:21:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: kaanapali-oneplus-iceland: panel I/O supply
 at stock's 1.9 V

The panel driver enables vddio (L12B) without asking for a voltage, so the
rail ran at the lower bound of its range, 1.8 V. Stock's power list for
this panel asks for 1.9 V at 220 mA, and that is what the rail shows under
stock Android with the screen on.

The LDO moves in 8 mV steps; 1.896 V is the step at stock's value that
stays within stock's maximum of 1.9 V. Make it the only voltage.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
index 7f1b07c82af6..073eaee1fd56 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/kaanapali-oneplus-iceland.dts
@@ -634,10 +634,13 @@
 						   RPMH_REGULATOR_MODE_AUTO>;
 		};
 
-		/* OEM change for Display */
+		/*
+		 * The panel's I/O supply. Stock's range is 1.2 .. 1.9 V and its panel power list asks for
+		 * 1.9 V; 1.896 V is the regulator step that does not exceed that.
+		 */
 		vreg_l12b_1p9: ldo12 {
 			regulator-name = "vreg_l12b_1p9";
-			regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;//OEM set to 1.2V, but it was too low
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <1896000>;
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1896000>;
 			regulator-initial-mode = <RPMH_REGULATOR_MODE_AUTO>;
 			regulator-allow-set-load;
